2026-09-28  Frédéric Mahé  <frederic.mahe@cirad.fr>
        * Version 2.2.0 (in development)
        * New option --version: shows the version and the reference, and
          exits with status 0
        * Fix: --help exited with status 1: it now exits with status 0,
          as other GNU-style programs (KI-2)
        * Fix: with tblastn and tblastx (-p 3, -p 4), the simple XML output
          (-m 7) reported the length of the translated database sequence
          (<len>, in amino acids), whereas the other outputs report its
          length in nucleotides. It is now in nucleotides too (KI-36)

2026-09-27  Frédéric Mahé  <frederic.mahe@cirad.fr>
        * Version 2.1.2
        * The build date and time are no longer shown in the help message,
          the plain output (-m 0) and the tabular output with comments
          (-m 9): two builds of the same sources are now identical
          (reproducible builds)
        * Fix: the long option --taxidlist, shown in the help message and
          in the README, was rejected: only --taxid was accepted (KI-1).
          Both names are now accepted (--taxid is kept as an alias)
        * Fix: an empty line at the beginning of the query file was read
          as an empty query, and all the other queries were silently
          ignored (KI-18)
        * Fix: with -v 0 -b 0, an element before the start of the (empty)
          hit list was read (KI-10). No hits are reported, as before
        * Fix: query lines longer than 2,047 characters were read in
          chunks: the end of a long header was read as sequence (KI-16),
          and a '>' at position 2,048 of a sequence line started a new
          query (KI-17). Lines of any length are now read whole
        * Fix: carriage returns (Windows line endings) are removed from
          query headers (KI-21)
        * Fix: with the simple XML output (-m 7), hits shown without an
          alignment (beyond -b) reported an uninitialized sequence length
          (<len>), which made the output non-deterministic (KI-37)
        * Fix: bytes above 0x7f (e.g. UTF-8 characters) in query files and
          score matrix files were read as negative indexes, out of the
          bounds of the symbol tables (KI-19). They are ignored, as other
          unknown symbols
        * Fix: databases with PDB identifiers made by current versions of
          makeblastdb (e.g. pdbaa, nr) could not be searched nor dumped
          ("Unexpected object a3", "Error parsing binary ASN.1"): the
          chain-id field of PDB identifiers is now read, and chain names
          are shown as they are by BLAST+ (e.g. pdb|4JKL|Lb) (KI-22)
        * Fix: gap penalties with a sum of 128 or more (gap open + gap
          extension), and matrix scores or nucleotide penalties below
          -128, wrapped around in the 7-bit search engine: wrong scores,
          or "Internal error in align function." (KI-11, KI-12). They are
          now clamped in the 7-bit engine only, which is exact; the other
          engines use the real values
        * Fix: matrix scores or nucleotide rewards from 32,768 to 65,535,
          scores below -32,768, and gap penalties above 32,767 did not fit
          in the 16-bit search engine: hits were lost or scores were wrong
          (KI-11, KI-12, KI-13). The 16-bit engine is now skipped for such
          values (the 63-bit engine is used, slower but exact)
        * Fix: the dump (-N) of a database with the tblastn or tblastx
          symbol types (-p 3, -p 4) printed the translated sequence with
          the nucleotide alphabet ('###'). The nucleotide sequence is now
          dumped (KI-24)
        * Fix: in the ParAlign XML output (-m 99), search start and end
          times were printed after they were freed ("(null)" with glibc)
          (KI-28)
        * Fix: in the ParAlign XML output (-m 99), blastn hits on the minus
          strand were reported on the plus strand, and their anchors were
          not unique when both strands of a sequence matched (KI-29)
        * Fix: special characters (& < > " ') are escaped in the XML
          outputs (-m 7 and -m 99): query ids and descriptions, database
          sequence descriptions, links, file names, database title and
          score matrix name. Descriptions such as "5'-3' exonuclease" or
          "A&B" produced invalid XML (KI-27)
        * Fix: strings of 2,048 characters or more in database headers
          (e.g. long titles) were stored without room for their final
          null byte, which was written one byte past the end of the
          buffer (KI-38). Outputs are unchanged
        * Fix: in score matrix files, a row with fewer scores than
          columns was accepted, and the missing scores took the value of
          the previous score (KI-14). Such rows are now rejected
          ("Problem parsing score matrix file.")
        * Fix: only spaces ended the query id: with a tab in the query
          header (e.g. ">q1<TAB>description"), the tabular outputs (-m 8,
          -m 9) had an extra column, and the XML output (-m 7) showed the
          description in the query id (KI-20). Any whitespace character
          now ends the query id, as in BLAST
        * Fix: the error message for a missing database sequence file
          (.psq, .nsq) was followed by an empty line (KI-35)
        * Fix: typo in the parameter block of the plain output (-m 0),
          "Effecive db size" is now "Effective db size" (KI-34)
        * Fix: with an illegal symbol type above 5 (e.g. -p 6), the error
          message was "Illegal gap penalties." (no default gap penalties
          for such types). The symbol type is now checked before the gap
          penalties: "Illegal symbol type." (KI-5)
        * Fix: tblastx (-p 4) refused --strand 2 ("Illegal strand
          specified for protein query."), although its query is a
          nucleotide sequence. The minus strand of the query can now be
          searched alone (KI-4)
        * Fix: the output file (--out) was created, or truncated, before
          the other options were checked: a typo in another option
          destroyed the previous content of the file (KI-8). It is now
          opened once all options are checked
        * Fix: numerical option values were not checked (atol, atof):
          trailing characters were ignored (-a 2abc was read as 2),
          non-numerical values were read as zero (-v abc), and -z
          7.06e+06 was read as 7 (KI-9, GitHub #9). Values are now
          checked: non-numerical values, trailing characters and values
          out of range are rejected with the error message of the
          option. -z accepts the real notation (7.06e+06 is 7,060,000),
          but not fractional values. Negative values of -v, -b, -u and
          -k are rejected, as well as -c values below 1 (a minimum score
          of 0 or less ended with "Internal error in align function.")
        * Fix: unknown names of symbol types (e.g. -p blastpp or
          -p BLASTP) silently selected blastn (symbol type 0), and
          unknown strand names were read as 0 (KI-3). They are now
          rejected ("Illegal symbol type.", "Illegal query strands
          specified.")
        * Fix: an expect value (-e) of zero, negative or non-numerical
          disabled the E-value filter: all hits were reported (KI-7).
          Such values are now rejected ("Illegal expect value
          specified."). Score thresholds computed from E-values are also
          protected against infinite values (e.g. an empty database)
        * Fix: taxid list files (-x) were read with fscanf(): negative
          values became huge values, huge values made the allocation of
          the taxid bitmap fail ("Unable to allocate enough memory."),
          and reading stopped silently at the first non-numerical value
          (e.g. "abc", or "9606,10090": only 9606 was read) (KI-25).
          Taxids must now be decimal numbers below 2^31, separated by
          whitespace (usually one per line); any other value stops swipe
          with its line number ("Illegal taxid on line N of taxid file
          FILE.")
        * mpiswipe (the MPI version of SWIPE) is deprecated: it is no
          longer built by "make" ("make mpiswipe" builds it), its help
          message says so, and it will be removed in version 2.2.0
        * Build: "make" builds swipe only (mpiswipe is opt-in). The
          Makefile honours CXX, CXXFLAGS, CPPFLAGS and LDFLAGS (user
          flags come after swipe's own flags), compiles with -std=c++11
          and -pthread, tracks header dependencies, has install and
          uninstall targets (PREFIX, DESTDIR), a complete clean target,
          and build recipes: release (default), DEBUG=1 (sanitizers,
          extended warnings), COVERAGE=1, PROFILE=1 and TRACE=1
        * Build: no compilation warnings with -Wall -Wextra, with GCC
          4.8.5 to 15 and clang 20; several of the fixed warnings were
          potential buffer overflows (ASN.1 parser, matrix reader)
        * Fix: in the ParAlign XML output (-m 99), the ungapped
          statistical parameters (lambda, kappa, eta) were the gapped
          ones (KI-31). They are now read from the ungapped rows of the
          NCBI tables (e.g. BLOSUM62: 0.3176, 0.134, 0.4012)
        * Fix: in the ParAlign XML output (-m 99), the query file name
          was prefixed with "./" (".//tmp/q.fa", "./-" for the standard
          input) (KI-32). It is now shown as given
        * Fix: in the ParAlign XML output (-m 99), sound queries (-p 5)
          were described as nucleotides, with an empty sequence, and
          their database as amino acids (KI-30). Both are now described
          as "Sound", and the query sequence is shown
        * Fix: the elapsed time was measured with times(), in clock ticks
          (10 ms): small searches reported "Speed: inf GCUPS", empty
          queries "nan GCUPS", and the speed of sound searches (-p 5)
          was not multiplied by the query length (KI-33). The elapsed
          time is now measured with a monotonic clock
          (std::chrono::steady_clock), the speed is "n/a" when no time
          elapsed, and 0 for empty queries
        * Fix: the sizes of the database files were not checked: a
          truncated index file (.pin, .nin) was read beyond its end and
          taken as an empty database, and offsets beyond the ends of the
          header and sequence files could be used (KI-23). The header
          and offset tables of the index file, and the offsets, are now
          checked ("Database index file FILE is truncated or
          corrupted.", and the same for header and sequence files)

